.TH CJSON_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_declare_scoped \- declare a cjson variable with automatic destruction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson_declare_scoped(var_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a cjson handle named var_name, sets it to NULL and attaches
a cleanup attribute to it.  When var_name goes out of scope, the
cleanup frees the tree that the variable holds, exactly as
cjson_destroy() does.  It runs at the end of the enclosing block on
every way out of it: a normal exit, break, continue, return and
goto.

Only store a tree that the caller owns in the variable, such as the
result of cjson_parse() or of a cjson_create_*() function.  A
borrowed reference from cjson_get() must not go there, because the
cleanup would free it while its parent holds it.

The macro relies on a GCC and Clang extension.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
var_name  Name of the variable to declare.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    cjson_declare_scoped(root);
    root = cjson_parse("{\e"k\e":1}", NULL);
    /* root is freed automatically at the closing brace */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_declare (3),
.BR cjson_destroy (3)
